It is likely the `sym' driver that called bus_dma_map_load() with the
bogus value, but it may well have received it as the length for an I/O
from some upper layer.

Only part of value is for sure bogus. The low 16 bits are possibly ok
(0x8000=3D32K). Some 16 bit (short) counter accessed as 32 bit value will
give you 16 stale bits as upper bits on little-endian machines, for
example.
Unfortunately, I haven't time at the moment for compiling the kernel -Wall
and inspect all warnings messages that may let think about such a mistyped
access. Btw, having 0 warnings using -Wall without using wild casts to
hide them would help a lot. Is this in the 5.0 todo list? ;-)

